  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 307 AM CDT Mon Aug 3 2026\n\nToday:\n\nAn upper level ridge will provide dry weather across the Upper \nMississippi River Valley with high temperatures ranging from \nthe upper 70s to the mid-80s.\n\nTonight through Tuesday Night:\n\nThe 03.00z models continue to show a shortwave trough moving along\nthe US/Canadian border tonight through Tuesday night, flattening\nthe upper level ridge over the Upper Mississippi River Valley in\nthe process. This will allow a Canadian cold front to move \nslowly southeast through the Upper Mississippi River Valley on \nTuesday. For areas along and north of the Interstate 90 \ncorridor, this front will move into the area late tonight and \nTuesday morning. This timing is not that favorable for \nwidespread showers and storms. As a result, rainfall amounts \nwill likely be less than a tenth of an inch north of Interstate \n90.\n\nOn Tuesday afternoon and night, low level CAPE (0-1 km) up to \n1500 J/kg will lie along and ahead of the cold front in \nnortheast Iowa and southwest into central Wisconsin. With the \nbetter 0-3 km and 0-6 km shear trailing the front, organized \nsevere weather appears unlikely at this time. \n\nWhile the severe weather threat looks low, there is some concern for \nheavy rain with mean winds parallel to the frontal boundary and \nprecipitable water values around 2 inches. Resultant training \nthunderstorms are therefore possible in the vicinity of the \nfrontal boundary. Rainfall rates in excess of 1 inch per hour \nare possible at times with highest overall amounts likely \nranging from 1 to 3 inches locally in in northeast Iowa and \nsouthwest Wisconsin.\n\nLow temperatures will range from the lower 50s to mid-60s on Monday \nand Tuesday nights. High temperatures will range from the mid-70s to \nlower 80s on Tuesday.\n\nWednesday into Thursday:\n\nAnother shortwave trough shifts east through the Dakotas and \nthe Upper Mississippi River Valley Wednesday into Thursday. An\ninitially quasi-stationary front draped across northeast Iowa \nand southwest Wisconsin on Wednesday will gradually move \nsoutheast of the area on Wednesday night and Thursday. With \nlittle collocated shear, organized convection appears unlikely \nat this time.\n\nThe vertical lift across across northeast Iowa and southwest \nWisconsin will be enhanced by the right entrance region of a 110-\nknot 250 jet on Wednesday afternoon. Precipitable water values\nnearing 2 inches may again result in rainfall rates of 1\"+ per\nhour and potential for localized flooding in urban areas.\n\nHigh temperatures will be in the 70s and lower 80s on Wednesday. Low \ntemperatures will be in the 50s on Wednesday night. High \ntemperatures will range from the mid-70s to mid-80s on Thursday.\n\nThis Weekend into Early Next Week\n\nThe northern extent of the upper level ridge is highly uncertain. \nThe GEFS suggests high temperatures will be in the 70s and 80s. \nMeanwhile, the ECMWF suggests that there will be the potential for \nhigh temperatures to potentially range from the mid-80s to mid-90s \nwith heat indices ranging from the lower 90s to around 100.",
